nt yet  to be disrupted  to understand why no one wants to take  on a TNT we first have to understand how  the company got so big in the first  place and this story dates all the way  back to 1875 to a man that you're  probably familiar with Alexander Graham  Bell if you're not familiar with this  guy he's the dude who invented the  telephone and like any good inventor  Graham Bella moved to monetizer's  invention by connecting the world to do  this he created a company called The  Bell Telephone Company with the two of  his buddies Thomas Watson and Gardner  green Hubbard right after he invented  the telephone the telephone was  basically an instant success and this is  not really surprising at first you  couldn't make calls from New York to  California but just being able to make  calls within NYC alone was insane you no  longer had to send a postcard or travel  30 or 45 minutes just to pass a message  instead it you could adjust telephone  the person from the comfort of your home  or office for the first few decade the  Bell company was just a conglomeration  of dozens of smaller networks spread  across the U.S but at the turn of the  century the Bell company evolved into  something much bigger thanks to a new  invention called loaded coils that could  carry signals across much longer  distances suddenly all of the smaller  networks across the U.S could start  connecting with each other to form a one  ginormous Network and Bell Telephone  would have changed their name to atnt or  American Telephone and Telegraph to  reflect this new National Focus this was  by no means an easy feat though as it  took them another 15 years to actually  make this Vision a reality but  eventually in January of 1915 Alexander  Graham Bell would be able to make a  ceremonious call from NYC to his  assistant Watson in San Francisco Graham  Bell would repeat the first words that  he ever cited through a telephone Mr  Watson come here I want you and Watson  would reply it will take me five days to  get there now as you would you guys the  crowds on both sides went absolutely  wild the mayors of the two cities would  go on to talk and even the President  Woodrow Wilson would put in a few words  we shouldn't note that this service was  insanely expensive coming in at 20.70  for the first three minutes followed by  6.35 cents for each additional minute I  mean that's expensive even in 20 23  dollars but if we adjust for inflation  we get 610 dollars for the first three  minutes followed by 200 per minute after  that obviously the average person could  not afford this but for a rich  businessmen this was an invaluable  service you were literally able to cut  down on 10 days worth of back and forth  travel just to communicate but not  everyone was so happy if at T was able  to connect California and New York that  meant that they also owned everything in  between it and Regulators weren't not  very happy with that they had let this  light for quite some time as they were  busy cracking down on Standard Oil but  after the breakup of Standard Oil in  1911 Regulators would shift their entire  Focus onto ATT  first of the government crackdowns came  in 1913 with something called the  Kingsbury commitment at the time at T  not only controlled the entire network  but they also produced the only viable  telephone fortunately for atnt the  government wasn't looking to destroy the  entire company with this settlement they  simply wanted to encourage competition  within both the network space and the  telephone space and they would soon come  to a pretty favorable settlement  consisting of three stipulations first  atnt had to allow other companies to use  their long distance Network for  commercial purposes second at T had to  spin off their telephone company into a  separate company called Western Electric  and at three at T was not allowed to  purchase any other company without the  government's approval the government  hoped that the new companies wouldn't  jump onto this opportunity but no one  did for the next 40 years and honestly  this isn't even att's fault even if at  themselves it truly encouraged  competition no one is going to compete  against them simply because it's not a  very appealing business here's the thing  as an entrepreneur during this time  period you could either produce World  War II materials jump onto the  automotive Trend or create a retail  giant or you could try to build out a  national network and take on ATT the  problem isn't that ATT is a monopoly the  problem is that the capital time and  effort required to build a network of  that skill simply isn't worth it when  there are so many better opportunities  and this is still very much True to this  day I mean would you rather build a 6G  company and try to take on a TNT or  would you rather build a social media  app and try to take on Facebook while  your odds of success are super low with  both Endeavors your odds of success in  social media are likely magnitudes  higher than your odds in  telecommunications the government  eventually came to this same realization  and they would become even more  aggressive with their crackdowns in 1949  the government would sue at T once again  and this lawsuit would be dragged on for  seven years eventually they would come  to a new settlement with three more  stipulations first ATT had to sell  Western Electric to a third party second  it ATT was barred from expanding their  business outside of connecting telephone  calls and finally at T had licensed  their patents to potential competitors  but due to all the reasons that we just  discussed no one still wanted to get  into the Telecommunications space all of  this eventually culminated into a  boiling point in 1982 when Regulators  ordered that atnt be split up into seven  different companies and seems like the  government was pretty confident in this  move in fact they were so confident that  they would even remove the stipulations  of the 1956 agreement meaning that the  new smaller companies could expand and  dim new lines of business ironically  though this this ended up backfiring big  time as this simply allowed ATT to  expand into broadband internet and  Wireless Communications the two sectors  that have allowed them to survive  through all of this the one thing that  becomes extraordinarily clear is that  you can't kill monopolies simply using  regulation or even breakups the only way  to kill monopolies is through  revolutionary Innovation take standard  oil for example they were literally  broken up over a hundred years ago yet  there's still a top 10 company worth 450  billion dollars in the form of Exxon the  reason for this is that people's demand  for oil never changed in fact it only  went up the same thing could be said  about ATT for much of the 1900s the  Government tried everything they could  to stop at T other than straight up  shutting down the company but none of  this addressed the insatiable demand for  communications something that did  address this demand however was the  internet and cell phones but somehow the  government managed to break up atnt at  the perfect time for them to switch to  supporting the internet and cell phones  if the government had and done that at T  would have gotten destroyed given that  landline popularity has plummeted from  80 to 29 within the past 14 years but  instead it at T has been able to build a  new life with new sectors at this point  even Money Can't disrupt ATT and the  best example of this is Google's attempt  to take on ATT about 10 years ago Google  would tried to take on their broadband  internet Market by introducing it Google  Fiber at that time most Broadband  connections offered 10 maybe 20 Mbps per  second Max meanwhile Google was willing  to offer 1 000 Mbps or 1 gbps for  relatively affordable prices  first Google expanded to several cities  very quickly but it didn't take them  long to realize that they had bitten off  more than they could chew you see it as  soon as Google started introducing fiber  ATT hit back harder than they could ever  imagine at T turned around and spent 140  billion dollars on a nationwide rollout  of fiber and today they offer even  better speeds than Google coming in at  up to 5 gigabits per second now if  Google really wanted it they could go  all in on fiber and try to take on a TNT  but for what given that they would have  to build from scratch it would cost them  200 or 250 billion dollars even if a  bunch of people adopted this service and  Google Fiber grew to be as large as the  entire company of atnt it would still  just be a 130 billion dollar business  even if it became a 200 or 300 billion  dollar business that simply isn't worth  it when you're investing 200 to 250  billion dollars you're expecting it  trillions in return not hundreds of  billions not to mention it it's not even  an easy to manage simple business either  you literally have to deal with managing  hundreds of billions of dollars worth of  infrastructure just to make 10 margins  Google had a very similar realization  with their Wireless project Google fi as  well and if a company as powerful and as  rich as Google can take on a TNT no one  can  in the end ATT is one of those super  weird businesses that's worth owning it  if it already exists but not worth  building at this point the only way that  this company gets taken down it is if  another Communications Revolution comes  around and the company doesn't pivot now  of course maybe Verizon can slowly chip  away at their market share and slowly  drive them to unprofitability but that  doesn't really mean much given that rise  in it is also a product of the original  ATT with all of that being said though  we do have to give ATT prompts unlike  the leading tech companies that are  constantly trying to grow and reach the  next magnitude ATT has been very  comfortable with where they are they  know their Lane and they know it very  well and they 